-- Passage rg3-argument-evidence-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('24af91de-6170-5219-91ab-41f4cba9cba5', 'rg3-argument-evidence-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g3-argument-evidence'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Where Thunder Comes From$q$, $q$During a storm, you may see a flash of lightning. A few seconds later, you hear a loud boom. That boom is thunder. Where does it come from?

Lightning is a giant spark of electricity. When it shoots across the sky, it heats the air around it. In less than a second, that air gets hotter than the outside of the sun. As a result, the hot air spreads out very fast.

The air spreads out so fast that it pushes hard on the air next to it. That push travels out in waves called sound waves. When the waves reach your ears, you hear thunder.

How close the storm is changes the sound. If the lightning is close, the waves reach you quickly. Because they have not traveled far, you hear a sharp, loud crack. If the lightning is far away, you hear a soft, low rumble.

You always see lightning before you hear thunder. Light moves much faster than sound, so the flash reaches your eyes first. Try counting the seconds between the flash and the boom. About five seconds means the lightning is about one mile away.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
